#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1de6657-82b1-5bcb-b8cc-fd2629d31faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10832" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068371 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068372 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10849" :question/problem "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10997" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d9c51e-3a0e-5bc4-b172-8b6583ceb715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290008} {:answer/id #uuid "d90487e7-735b-56e9-bcca-77c3b5f97cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290009} {:answer/id #uuid "94b1405d-06d4-582a-9313-ba2f85b9a4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290010} {:answer/id #uuid "861f4686-f4df-53f0-ad82-ef638f2ee62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290011} {:answer/id #uuid "0891ba75-1555-5a7d-9417-da806933b1df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0891ba75-1555-5a7d-9417-da806933b1df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290012} :answer-field/id #uuid "859b6ae2-bd80-505f-a258-6fe94803aaa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f1d7bb9-17fc-5ea6-b46c-64a6f08a7c91" :question/math-academy-id "q-14253" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(1+2x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x+{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{y}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+C}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee68888-dbb2-5ca0-80cb-6c60f3bd0155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290050} {:answer/id #uuid "8545cda7-dff1-5955-a591-94cf845e0016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290051} {:answer/id #uuid "56c46223-d9f3-5e31-ae79-b5c2146f681f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290052} {:answer/id #uuid "f20b16d2-b13d-5314-a9d2-801a551d4be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290053} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb5bcff-df86-512b-b9bb-07304f9b353c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56c46223-d9f3-5e31-ae79-b5c2146f681f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290052} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf3d22a-e0e4-5060-a63d-8decf4600d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca7be506-83fc-5b5f-8d5e-a2372dca466d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158014" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200ff40b-c483-5511-ada4-9962f1c23d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290078} {:answer/id #uuid "549aa129-3c38-5279-aaf0-8fc6677cb0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290079} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6f447c-5fc8-5dfe-8140-683e78c870c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290080} {:answer/id #uuid "91acb97c-fd3b-5dfa-afb4-225be15bbf45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290081} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba46a08-ea30-56e0-baae-58f6e1ba67d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba46a08-ea30-56e0-baae-58f6e1ba67d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290082} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ebceda-4b19-5c0e-a39b-5accbe931e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb5bb3ab-6532-5577-9047-8f7af02e49c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-158026" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bae4517-ca75-59cb-a32f-b2a2a2c2eaa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290092} {:answer/id #uuid "0aeecf5d-5702-513e-801c-5c126e01c8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290093} {:answer/id #uuid "17ce1359-aa2a-5472-9c96-f2901f6cba5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290094} {:answer/id #uuid "222d92b3-2dcd-5344-b8b0-8d766efd687a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290095} {:answer/id #uuid "201dba49-412a-5f39-a491-3eaaf266d447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "201dba49-412a-5f39-a491-3eaaf266d447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290096} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0239e82-ea6b-51fb-b67b-3105cf1e47e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "575d49f3-8bd3-5463-bab9-a94f8bc81fb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158035" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & ={x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={x}^{3}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27ee6c2-1b6d-59c4-8c21-6fb69aefd4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290099} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1386bf-6e21-59b5-99bb-48e26b2dd99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290100} {:answer/id #uuid "025c3afe-0e1a-545c-93fc-afb7a94a47e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290101} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcabdb7-87f0-56d9-943a-24323eb049d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290102} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1a8c2a-acbb-54f7-810d-1ac1c25cb446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d27ee6c2-1b6d-59c4-8c21-6fb69aefd4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290099} :answer-field/id #uuid "b36339ba-910f-5f2b-aeb8-30257127d182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a9f1df5-0abe-52e3-bf22-4b38e70bcd85" :question/math-academy-id "q-158117" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128eee03-ebf6-5e0f-a12c-86ca6152676d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290106} {:answer/id #uuid "4bda40f0-2d83-59e6-8f7b-7ba05cf47032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290107} {:answer/id #uuid "61da996c-429e-5771-9273-3176fc625092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290108} {:answer/id #uuid "07c6ebfc-e491-5570-a132-4f1f7d478f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290109} {:answer/id #uuid "f72e1a12-1c41-5fc1-b88e-073808038329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c6ebfc-e491-5570-a132-4f1f7d478f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290109} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3632192-c66e-50c5-801e-6d1652a6c0f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76330838-614a-5353-8d92-b88e4c3411af" :question/math-academy-id "q-3850" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x{e}^{-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C).\n$$"}]}
